.TH DLIST_UNSHIFT 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
dlist_unshift \- Add a node to the head of a doubly linked list

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/dlist.h>

.IB void
.IB dlist_unshift(dlist* l, node* p);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBdlist_unshift\fR function adds a node \fBp\fR to the head of a doubly linked list \fBl\fR. If the list is initially empty, the new node becomes both the head and the tail of the list. Otherwise, the new node is prepended to the existing list and the head reference is updated accordingly.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB l
The doubly linked list to which the node is to be added.
.TP
.IB p
The node to be added to the head of the list.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function does not return a value.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBdlist_unshift\fR function:

.nf
#include <lib/dlist.h>

int main() {
    dlist l;
    dlist_init(&l);

    node* new_node = malloc(sizeof(node));
    dlist_node_init(new_node);
    // Initialize new_node with appropriate values

    dlist_unshift(&l, new_node); // Add new_node to the head of the list

    // Use the list as needed

    free(new_node); // Don't forget to free the node when done

    return 0;
}
.nef